WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.880
PRESENTER: The space pen can
write in zero gravity, which

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.920
is why astronauts have used it.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.370
It also writes underwater,
in extreme heat or cold,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.770
or when held upside down.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.840
All this is possible
due to the pen's

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.360
ingenious design that
keeps ink flowing

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.040
toward the tip no matter what.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.510
The space pen's ink is
pressurized with nitrogen.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.230
So unlike ordinary ballpoints,
it doesn't rely on gravity

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.330
to flow toward the tip.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:40.290
Invented in 1966, the
pen first went into space

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.050
with the Apollo 7 astronauts.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.080
The pen's writing point starts
out as a 3/10 of an inch

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.630
long block of stainless steel.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.050
It passes through more than a
dozen machining operations that

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.710
progressively shape a point
then bore a hole through the tip

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.920
to form a pocket for
the carbide steel ball.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.740
That makes this a ball point.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.930
The last station
inserts the ball

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.700
and curves the
edges of the pocket

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.370
inward so that the
ball is locked in yet

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.990
can rotate to spread ink.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.230
The replaceable ink cartridge,
called the "refill,"

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.020
begins as an empty brass tube.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.750
This assembly machine
inserts a white plastic ball

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:26.171
into the back end then
pumps in half a gram of ink.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.760
The white ball is
called the "float."

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.670
Its job is to follow
the ink down the tube,

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.310
moving residual ink
forward toward the point.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.380
Next, the machine
inserts the writing point

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.670
into the opposite
end of the tube,

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.150
then it crimps the end
to ensure the writing

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.140
point can't dislodge.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.330
Back to the other
end of the tube now,

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:58.900
the machine applies
a bit of sealant,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.920
injects nitrogen to pressurize
the refill, then caps the tube

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.210
with a hollow brass plug.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.919
Nitrogen is ideal for
pressurizing because it's

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:12.440
an inert gas that doesn't
harm the refill tube

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.250
or its contents.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.040
This demonstration shows
how the pressurized nitrogen

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.320
forces the ink flow.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.600
After subjecting each and
every refill to a writing test

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.240
and washing the surface to
remove traces of machine

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.940
lubricant and other
residues, a printer

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.740
applies the company
name and product

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:40.835
information on the refill.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.270
Certain space pen
models have a cap that

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.590
fits over the writing point.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.630
A feeder places a brass cap on
each poke of the cap assembly

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.770
machine which then pushes
the cap into position

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:56.610
to receive a clip.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.710
The clip is stamped
out of spring

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.520
steel, a fairly flexible metal.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.300
It's chrome-plated for corrosion
resistance and aesthetics.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.880
The machine drives
the clip's teeth

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.330
through the wall of
the cap then curls them

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.220
back toward the
inside of the wall,

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.525
locking the clip in position.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.820
To prepare the two-part
brass body of the pen,

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.860
a feeder drops the
bottom part, called

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.980
the "barrel," onto each poke
of the barrel assembly machine.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.360
To straighten the
writing end, the machine

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:36.590
inserts a brass reinforcement
piece called the "nose tip."

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:40.610
It then crimps the end, flaring
the nose tip inside the barrel,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:43.070
so that it becomes wider
than the barrel opening

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.770
and, therefore, can't slip out.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:49.550
This model has a chrome-plated
brass body and clipless cap.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:54.080
To begin assembling the pen,
workers place the barrel

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.390
in a foam holder,
insert a spring

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:59.355
to keep the refill in position,
then a threaded connector

00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:00.155
made of brass.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.480
The barrel goes
through a machine

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.370
which puts a silicone
rubber O ring

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:08.585
on the top edge of the barrel.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.330
And now the final assembly.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.870
They place a refill
in the barrel,

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:19.070
insert the connector into
the top half of the body,

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:22.084
then, with an electric motor,
thread the parts together.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.170
After placing the cap open
side up in a foam holder,

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.180
they insert the pen.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.965
The O ring holds
the cap in place.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.080
The original space pen,
still in production,

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.620
has a push button on top to
push out the refill and another

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.640
on the side to retract it.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.210
This demonstration pen
has a cutaway section

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:45.335
to show the inner workings.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:54.320
All space pen models can write
underwater and in zero gravity.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:02.410
They also work in freezing cold,
intense heat, and upside down.

